{
  "contradictionVerdicts": [
    {
      "topic": "Which construct for platform services needing Context/DI, lifecycle, or test fakes: expect class vs common interface + platform binding",
      "verdict": "CONFIRMED",
      "reasoning": "All quotes verified in the files. ahmed models exactly the DI/lifecycle cases as expect class: line 178 `expect class SecureStorage` with Android actual line 185 `actual class SecureStorage(private val context: Context)` (Context = DI); lines 231-235 `expect class ConnectivityMonitor` with `startMonitoring()`/`stopMonitoring()` (lifecycle) and Android actual line 238 taking Context; lines 150-155 DatabasePathProvider + Context. The word 'interface' never appears as a boundary alternative anywhere in ahmed. chris directly opposes this: line 49 'If the operation needs an Activity, view controller, lifecycle owner, DI, or fakes, stop and use an interface supplied by platform code instead of an `expect class`', line 78 'Use interfaces when common code needs fakes, multiple implementations, runtime selection, or lifecycle ownership', and the boundary table line 26 maps 'injected dependencies, lifecycle ownership, runtime choice, or test fakes' to 'Common interface plus platform binding'. rcosteira line 13 'Prefer a common `interface` + per-platform binding over `expect class` whenever you need fakes / DI / lifecycle / runtime selection.' This is genuine opposing guidance on the identical decision for the identical service shapes (Context-injected, lifecycle-owning), not merely different depth."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Whether the platform boundary must be fakeable from common tests",
      "verdict": "CONFIRMED",
      "reasoning": "Verified. ahmed's only testing guidance (lines 404-414) tests `Platform.name` directly and states line 413 'Test runs on all platforms with actual implementations' — no fake, and because the service boundaries are expect/actual there is no injectable seam to substitute one. chris explicitly lists the opposite: mistakes table line 108 'No fakeable boundary for common tests | Use an interface instead of direct `expect` call', plus line 19 'testing common code with a fake where possible' and line 86 'Common tests use a fake.' rcosteira line 12 'a business `if`/`when` inside an actual belongs in common, tested with a fake.' For ahmed's stateful service classes (SecureStorage, ConnectivityMonitor) the fake-in-common approach chris/rcosteira prescribe is structurally impossible under the expect/actual design ahmed teaches, so this is a real methodological conflict. Minor nuance: chris's 'where possible' qualifier and its endorsement of expect/actual for simple compile-time values mean chris does not demand fakeability of trivial value boundaries like Platform.name itself; but for the service boundaries in question the opposition (real-actual-per-platform vs fake-in-common) is direct. The conflict partly overlaps and follows from contradiction 1."
    }
  ],
  "bugVerdicts": [
    {
      "description": "expect/actual property type mismatch on ConnectivityMonitor.isOnline: expect `val isOnline: Flow<Boolean>` vs actuals `actual val isOnline: StateFlow<Boolean>`",
      "verdict": "CONFIRMED",
      "reasoning": "Verified: line 232 `val isOnline: Flow<Boolean>` in the expect class; line 240 (Android) and line 268 (iOS) both `actual val isOnline: StateFlow<Boolean> = _isOnline.asStateFlow()`. Kotlin requires an actual member's declared type to match the expected declaration's type exactly; there is no return-type/property covariance relaxation for expect/actual. Even though StateFlow<Boolean> is a subtype of Flow<Boolean>, the declared property types differ, producing an 'actual property has different type than expected' error. Real major compile bug."
    },
    {
      "description": "FileSystem Android actual references undefined symbol `context`",
      "verdict": "CONFIRMED",
      "reasoning": "Verified lines 67-77: `actual class FileSystem {` declares no constructor parameter and no `context` property, yet the bodies use `context.filesDir.absolutePath` (line 69) and `context.cacheDir.absolutePath` (lines 72, 75). The corresponding expect (line 60) also declares no constructor. `context` is an unresolved reference in every method body — real major compile error."
    },
    {
      "description": "expect/actual constructor mismatch: expect classes have implicit no-arg constructors while Android actuals add a required Context primary-constructor parameter",
      "verdict": "CONFIRMED",
      "reasoning": "Verified. SecureStorage: expect line 178 `expect class SecureStorage {` (no constructor -> implicit no-arg) vs Android actual line 185 `actual class SecureStorage(private val context: Context)`. DatabasePathProvider: expect line 150 vs actual line 155 `(private val context: Context)`. ConnectivityMonitor: expect line 231 vs actual line 238 `(private val context: Context)`. Each expect class exposes only an implicit no-arg constructor; the Android actuals provide only a one-arg (Context) constructor with no matching no-arg, so no actual constructor matches the expected one -> compile error. Context also cannot be added to the commonMain expect, which is precisely the constraint that drives chris/rcosteira toward interfaces. The iOS actuals (lines 162, 210, 266) correctly have no constructor, so the finding's Android-only scope is accurate. Real major bug."
    },
    {
      "description": "iosMain actuals contain Swift syntax, not valid Kotlin/Native (Swift string interpolation and leading-dot enum)",
      "verdict": "CONFIRMED",
      "reasoning": "Verified. Line 25 `actual val name: String = \"iOS \\(UIDevice.currentDevice.systemVersion)\"` uses Swift interpolation `\\(...)`; in Kotlin `\\(` is an illegal escape sequence (Kotlin uses `$`), a compile error. Line 276 `_isOnline.value = monitor.currentPath.status == .satisfied` uses Swift leading-dot enum shorthand `.satisfied`, which is not valid Kotlin (an expression cannot begin with a bare dot). Both are real compile errors; classified major."
    },
    {
      "description": "Logger iosMain block has unbalanced braces; the class is never closed",
      "verdict": "CONFIRMED",
      "reasoning": "Verified lines 308-322. Line 309 `actual class Logger {` and line 310 `actual fun log(...) {` open two scopes; line 314 `}` closes only the `log` function; line 316 declares `private fun LogLevel.toOSLogType() = when (this) {` and line 321 `}` closes only the `when`. Three opening braces (class, fun, when) vs two closing — the class brace opened at 309 is never matched, so the source is syntactically unbalanced and will not compile. The Android Logger (lines 297-306) by contrast is correctly closed. Real major bug."
    },
    {
      "description": "Self-contradictory DO advice: '// Provide default implementations when possible' above a bodyless expect class Analytics, when expect declarations cannot carry bodies",
